¶
'''adumbrant''' (ad-um′brant), ''a.'' [< L. ''adumbran(t-)s'', ppr. of ''adumbrare'': see ''adumbrate.''] Giving a faint shadow, or showing a slight resemblance.

'''adumbrate''' (ad-um′brāt), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''adumbrated'', ppr. ''adumbrating''. [< L. ''adumbratus'', pp. of ''adumbrare'', cast a shadow over; in painting, to represent an object with due mingling of light and shadow, also represent in outline; < ''ad'', to, + ''umbra'', shadow.] '''1.''' To overshadow; partially darken or conceal.
{{smaller block|<poem>Nor did it [a veil] cover, but ''adumbrate'' only
Her most heart-piercing parts.</poem>
{{right|''Marlowe and Chapman'', Hero and Leander, iv.}}}}
'''2.''' Figuratively, to give a faint shadow or resemblance of; outline or shadow forth; foreshadow; prefigure.
{{smaller block|Both in the vastness and the richness of the visible universe the invisible God is ''adumbrated.'' {{right|''Is. Taylor.''}}}}
{{smaller block|In truth, in every Church those who cling most tenaciously to the dogma are just the men “who have least hold of the divine substance” which it faintly ''adumbrates.''
{{right|''H. N. Oxenham'', Short Studies, p. 314.}}}}

'''adumbration''' (ad-um-brā′shọn), ''n.'' [< L. ''adumbratio(n-)'', < ''adumbrare'': see ''adumbrate.''] '''1.''' The act of adumbrating or making a shadow or faint resemblance.—'''2.''' Figuratively, a faint sketch; an imperfect representation; something that suggests by resemblance, or shadows forth; a foreshadowing.
{{smaller block|Our knowledge is . . . at best a faint confused ''adumbration.'' {{right|''Glanville'', Scep. Sci.}}}}
{{smaller block|Belief comes into existence when man is not reasonable enough to have a theory about anything, while he is still mainly a feeling animal, possessing only some ''adumbra-''}}

