{{smaller block|<poem>Now Morn, her rosy steps in the eastern clime
''Advancing'', sow’d the earth with orient pearl.</poem>{{right|''Milton'', P. L., v. 2.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>One lac’d the helm, another held the lance:
A third the shining buckler did ''advance''.</poem>{{right|''Dryden'', Pal. and Arc., l. 1732.}}}}
{{smaller block|A line was entrenched, and the troops were ''advanced'' to the new position. {{right|''U. S. Grant'', Pers. Mem., I. 377.}}}}
'''2.''' To forward in time; accelerate: as, to ''advance'' the growth of plants.—'''3.''' To improve or make better; benefit; promote the good of: as, to ''advance'' one’s true interests.
{{smaller block|As the calling dignifies the man, so the man much more ''advances'' his calling. {{right|''South'', Sermons}}}}
'''4.''' To promote; raise to a higher rank: as, to ''advance'' one from the bar to the bench.
{{smaller block|<poem>And to ''advance'' again, for one man’s merit,
A thousand heirs that have deserved nought?</poem>{{right|''Sir J. Davies'', Immortal. of Soul, viii.}}}}
{{smaller block|It has ben the fate of this obliging favorite to ''advance'' those who soone forget their original. {{right|''Evelyn'', Diary, July 22, 1674.}}}}
